WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.300
- I next have to invite myself up

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:15.210
to give a brief chat about
visual culture computation

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.890
in the Digital Humanities lab,

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.860
and what we've been doing
over the last couple years.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.270
Almost everything I'm gonna show today

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.980
in my talk is actually a team effort.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.890
It involves user experience
design from Monica Ong Reed,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.020
it involves some incredibly
sophisticated programming

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.420
from Doug Duhaime from
whom you'll hear later.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.400
It involves a lot of
planning organization advice

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.573
feedback from Cathy DeRose from
whom you'll also hear later.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.400
So I just wanna preface that by saying

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.160
what I'm gonna show
you today is the result

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.930
of a laboratory coming together

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.360
of trying to build reusable for folks

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.260
in the cultural heritage space.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.210
And so that's what I'm
gonna focus on today,

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.820
focus on the problem of
visual culture computation

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.180
and I think, if I'm not misremembering,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.780
I think this is actually a
term that Catherine Haskins,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.320
who's in the audience today
asked us to think about.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.730
My own background as well as that of Doug,

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.500
and Cathy and others in the lab

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.530
is actually really
textural, so we have degrees

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.660
in literature of some kind and
coming to Yale for all of us

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.140
was this really amazing experience

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.600
because we started to think about

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.080
the world-class physical holdings

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.800
that included oil paintings
and medieval manuscripts

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.140
and sculptures and things
which would never be reducible

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:30.690
down to Unicode text.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.760
So all the things I
had done on my post-doc

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.530
of text mining Google Books,
suddenly I'm in a world

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.280
where I've got RGB pixel values,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.070
I've got 3D data from sculptures,

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.220
it was really exciting challenge

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.120
that Catherine and others,
including our Dean of Humanities

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.480
laid out for us in the
lab, to be responsive

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.130
to visual culture and so
what I'm gonna show you today

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.470
is gonna be some steps towards that.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.303
What I'm really gonna try

00:01:52.303 --> 00:01:55.000
to focus on is the problem of scale.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.530
So the notion of
collection-scale analysis,

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.920
collection-scale experience
is really important to us.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.620
And that's because I think
none of us want to interfere

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.010
with the traditional art historical praxis

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:10.710
of sitting in front of an
amazing oil painting at YCBA

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.260
or looking at archival photographs,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.060
that's how I was trained
as an undergraduate

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.660
in our history, that will always go on.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.350
What we're trying to enable
with visual culture computation

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.170
is methods and approaches
which are impossible

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.513
once you reach the scale of 10s
or 100s of 1000s of objects.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.260
And in order to start this conversation

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.870
I wanna give my very
basic attempt at talking

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.900
about some of the principles
of machine vision.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:37.990
There's really no better
person in this room

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.260
than me, the PhD in Swedish literature

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.850
to talk about machine vision

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.640
and deep convolutional
networks, I can't tell you

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.250
how much I'm looking forward to this.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.940
But what I wanna start is
with an interactive thing,

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.820
is if you have a cell
phone, usually people say,

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.610
put away the cell phone,
take out your Android,

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.540
take out your iPhone and just
go to your photo library.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.420
And doesn't matter if you use Android

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.300
or you use Google Photos, if
you have photos on your phone,

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.760
doesn't matter, you'll
either find a search box

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.790
at the top or bottom left,
sometimes you have to pull down.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.660
I'm just gonna recommend
that you type the word cat.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.800
Cats are my favorite example for this

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.150
and in my example I have
254 pictures of cats.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.970
These are generally my sister's cat,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.320
sometimes they're librarians
cats here at Yale.

00:03:20.387 --> 00:03:22.050
And if you don't have a
cat you could try dog,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.940
you could try kid, you
could try car, bicycle,

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.420
whatever you think you
might take pictures of.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.880
No matter if you use Google Photos,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.290
if you use IOS, you're gonna find

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.310
what you're looking for I hope.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:35.750
And you can ask then what's going on?

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.560
And why am I seeing pictures
that I have never labeled?

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.890
This isn't Flickr, I haven't
described these things,

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.600
I haven't hired a processing archivist

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.180
from the Beinecke to
expertly describe this.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.290
So who decided these were
cats and how did that happen?

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.250
And the answer of
course, is that all of us

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.417
on our phones or in the cloud depending on

00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:56.020
how your architecture is,
Google does it in the cloud,

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:57.640
Apple does it on the phone.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.270
There's a convolutional neural network,

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.690
which is captioning or
describing everything

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.120
you take a picture of.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.370
And this is really powerful,
it means I don't have to

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.550
go and label my pictures,
in fact, I don't even think

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.077
there is a way to label my
pictures on my cell phone, right?

00:04:10.077 --> 00:04:13.060
You take a picture and you
can then search for it.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.890
So this is very powerful, very exciting

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.440
and all of this, although
theorized many years

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:18.990
or even decades previously really

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.530
only hit the real world around 2012

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:24.470
when a convolutional
neural network was able to

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:28.050
solve a image description
challenge in a way

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.600
that was significantly better than

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.563
other image captioning algorithms in 2011.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.350
And so you can see around
2012 that something changed

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.550
and it meant that we started
getting these systems,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.160
which trained on categories
like cat and dog and chair

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.980
and bagel and banana would be
able to describe those things.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.750
And so this is what's running
now in all of our cell phones

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:50.760
whether we want it to or not.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.090
So this is really exciting, why don't we

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.160
take these systems which can describe cat

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.830
so effectively, which can
find bagels and skateboards,

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.960
why don't we take these
machine vision systems

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.440
from companies like Google
or Amazon or Facebook

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.530
and let them loose on
our cultural heritage?

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:09.303
There's truly nothing that
could go wrong here, right?

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.330
And so I just got back from a brief talk

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.730
in Sydney in Australia
and so I was interested

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.390
in this idea of let's use machine vision

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:20.780
to catalog Australia's visual heritage,

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.940
so I went to an Australian history website

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.170
and I took an image, the first one I saw,

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:28.930
and I put it through probably
the best machine vision API,

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:31.770
the Google Cloud Vision
API, this is absolutely,

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.860
100% gonna give me the right answer.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:35.980
So I gave it the image, and I said,

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.130
could you describe what this is?

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:39.500
And it told me that it
was a fashion accessory,

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.510
probably jewelry, with little

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.420
less certainty possibly earrings,

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.303
but maybe body jewelry or silver.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.530
So let's actually go to the source

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:49.520
and figure out what these are.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.320
Well, these are leg manacles of course

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:55.160
from Australia's convict
history and actually the fact

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.010
that Google would recognize them as such

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:58.800
is not something that I invented,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.110
it's something that a curator in

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.930
the Smithsonian told me
based on some experience.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:06.580
You can only imagine the valence of this

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:10.500
in an American context with
African American slavery.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.160
So what happened, what went wrong?

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.050
Well, really, this isn't Google's fault,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:17.690
and this is not Amazon's
fault, it has to do with

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:20.370
the kinds of usage and captions that

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.930
off-the-shelf captioning
systems optimize for.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:24.830
They generally optimize for the cats

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:26.670
that I'm taking pictures of or the cars

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:30.960
or the bicycles, contemporary
images from 2019.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.010
Images which are captured on cell phones,

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.360
so we tend to take single
pictures of cute cats

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.360
on our cell phones, that's
what we use 'em for.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.900
And crucially I think
monetizable use-cases.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.120
It wouldn't surprise me if
Facebook has a recognizer

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.310
if I upload a picture of
myself drinking a Coke,

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.340
they can probably sell me an ad of Pepsi

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.700
next time I log in based
on some brand recognition,

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:50.330
that wouldn't surprise me at all.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.610
The training and the categories
make all the difference

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:56.590
and if there is bias, it's
in the training categories

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.500
and the captions and the data,

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.783
not necessarily inherently
in the linear algebra.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:07.780
This horrific justification
of a piece of metal

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.380
that is connected with a
convict or with a slave history

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.650
depending on which country you're in

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:16.050
is not the only unexpected
juxtaposition of visual culture

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.250
and in fact artists like Fred Wilson

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:20.210
working many years ago in the early '90s

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.430
at the Baltimore Country
Museum put together

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:26.450
a terrific exposition that
was called, Mining the Museum.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:29.330
And Mining the Museum is
exactly what Yale professor

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:30.940
in American studies and women's,

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:32.930
gender, and sexuality studies Laura Wexler

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.347
thought of when she saw this juxtaposition

00:07:35.347 --> 00:07:37.610
of convict shackles from Australia

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.170
being described as jewelry.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.410
She thought of Fred Wilson's installation

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.510
within Mining the Museum
called, "Metalwork."

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:45.590
And so I wanna acknowledge Laura's point

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.220
and I was able to find an
image of slave shackles

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:51.130
here we're in the American
context, the Southern context

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.000
at the bottom center of this image

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.610
juxtaposed by artist Fred
Wilson with some of the other

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:58.380
elements and material
culture from Baltimore

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:00.397
and Maryland broadly,
the production of which

00:08:00.397 --> 00:08:02.730
was of course enabled by slave labor.

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.930
So as we encounter these
unexpected or horrific

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.730
miscaptions in our
convolutional neural networks,

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:10.880
it is I think as we try to fix that,

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.530
worthwhile thinking about what such

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.630
unexpected juxtapositions
might trigger in our own minds

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.590
and I think that that
may be what Fred Wilson

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.113
was doing in an analog way
back in the early '90s.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:23.350
So as we turn away from some brief notions

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:26.190
of convolutional neural
networks in how they see

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:27.730
and how they describe the world,

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:30.000
I wanted to then move
on to three experiments

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.250
with visual culture
computation that try to get

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.230
around this problem,
that try to use the power

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:36.950
of what industry can give us whether

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:39.460
from Amazon or Facebook or Google.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.190
Take these pre-trained
networks and in some way

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:44.860
shift them and distort them to serve

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:47.490
the needs in cultural
heritage institutions,

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.860
perhaps natural science institutions,

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.860
what are the ways that we
can take the advancements

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.900
that are allowing me to
find all the cats I've ever

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:57.560
taken pictures of, but not
miscategorize the 19th century

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.280
or describe something in a
way that's very inappropriate.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.310
So we're gonna start with the first one,

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.910
which is specifically can
these pre-trained captioning

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:07.000
networks be useful
without their categories?

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.000
And in order to answer
that question we took

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.300
a collection which is actually split

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:14.100
between the Yale Beinecke Rare
Book and Manuscript Library

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.540
and the National Portrait
Gallery in the Smithsonian.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:19.490
The majority of the collection
physically is in the Beinecke

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:21.730
and then there's some very
important Mathew Brady negatives

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.990
that are at the National Portrait Gallery.

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:26.470
It's one of the largest private
collections of 19th century

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:28.930
photography from the 1860s to the 1890s,

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.340
the Civil War to the Gilded Age.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:34.170
And it's an incredibly rich
and powerful collection

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.780
to look through, it's famous
for its depictions of Lincoln.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.120
There's a problem though,
it doesn't include any cats.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.320
And as we know these
convolutional neural networks

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:43.310
are only good at finding cats.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:46.020
So we have this mismatch
between our source data

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.620
and the networks which are really good

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.160
at describing the world.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.200
And so the impetus behind
trying to fix that problem,

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:54.960
behind trying to find a
way that these networks

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.350
could still be useful, but in a world

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:00.240
where they weren't trying to
describe things too explicitly

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.880
has actually resulted in
this demonstration here,

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:04.810
so what I have here on the screen are

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:07.370
just a couple images from the
Meserve-Kunhardt Collection.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.120
There actually isn't too
much import in which ones

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.210
are on the screen right
now, this is a random sample

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:13.710
from our Civil War collection.

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.250
What maybe is a little
bit more interesting

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.870
is the ability to take this website

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:20.040
and do something with my mouse,

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.120
so I'm gonna move my mouse over
this dude here on the left.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.840
And when I move my mouse over him,

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:28.350
I'm gonna get images which
are visually similar to him

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:29.810
and when I move my mouse over this guy

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:32.480
I'm gonna get images which are
visually similar to that one,

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.040
move my mouse over a guy in an oval,

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:37.220
I'm gonna get images also
in that same oval vignette.

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:40.440
Let's go to another image, I
dunno if anybody likes boats,

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.660
we got some boats, like
houses, we got houses.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:46.000
Now in some cases these
are actual literal reprints

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.740
and that's okay, it's a large collection,

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:49.770
reprints were made for
a long period of time.

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:52.390
In some cases we know
what these things are,

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:54.060
we have captions and this happens

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.330
to be Lincoln's house in Springfield.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.080
Sometimes we know who this person was,

00:10:58.080 --> 00:10:59.890
we know it in one image
on the bottom right,

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:01.228
but not on the top left.

00:11:01.228 --> 00:11:02.830
So those are reprints, but what I think

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:05.000
is more interesting than the reprints

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:06.570
is the notion of the pictorial

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.650
conventions of depiction here.

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:11.470
So what I can do by surfing
through this website

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:13.330
is look at how people were depicted

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:16.020
in various ways based
on visual similarity.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.600
And the visual similarity
actually has a lot to do

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.270
with the convolutional
neural networks that we

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:23.950
were talking about before
and this project is based on

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:25.870
pre-trained convolutional neural networks

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:29.540
which are designed to find
skateboards, bagels, and cats.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.380
But in this artificial vision system

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.910
which starts with very simple discoveries

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:36.320
about maybe lines or small shapes

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:39.800
and evolves all the way into
this is a cat or this is a dog,

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:41.490
we step back from that final layer,

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:43.770
and we take the penultimate,
the semi-final layer

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:45.340
in the network, which gives us

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:48.240
about 2,048 abstract dimensions of sight.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.610
There's really no way to
describe these 2,048 ways

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:53.860
of seeing except that they are crucial

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:56.260
in distinguishing cats from skateboards.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:57.520
In that sense it kind of solves

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:58.970
the futurization problem for us,

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:00.400
we get a pre-trained model,

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:01.690
we shift it to do something else,

00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:03.980
we have this relatively
high dimensional space,

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:07.050
2,048 ways of seeing, so think about

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:10.708
one dimension of x, y, z, and
then 2,045 more dimensions

00:12:10.708 --> 00:12:12.620
that I don't have letters for.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:14.950
We can do math in that
high dimensional space.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:17.740
We can use a proximus
nearest neighbors algorithm

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.320
to find the images which are the closest

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.890
to a given image that
I'm moving my mouse over

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:25.030
in these other dimensions,
and that's what results

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:27.630
in all these other pages
of writing showing up,

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.290
perhaps because there's one dimension

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:31.270
which was responding to a white background

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:33.810
and another dimension which
was responding to squiggles,

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:36.120
put that together you
get basically all the

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:39.080
archival handwritten
documents in the collection.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.430
So we are basically taking
pre-trained networks,

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:43.870
but intercepting them
and using them to surface

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:46.493
other images of women in
sort of interesting ways.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.130
What I think this tool really gives you

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:51.400
is the kind of way to surf through,

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:54.150
by starting with one image
and discovering conventions

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:57.680
of portraiture in a time when
exposure times were long,

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:00.630
people took a good deal of
time getting dressed up,

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:03.310
sometimes their bodies were
even stabilized by metal stands,

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:04.810
so a lot of thought went into

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:06.360
these conventions of depiction.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.030
All right, so that is neural neighbors

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:12.830
and my favorite example from
this particular data set

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:15.790
which George Miles helped
bring to the Beinecke,

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:17.650
I call it the pugilist example,

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:20.120
so I've moved my mouse
over a boxer in the bottom

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:22.340
and I get all the other boxers,

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:24.810
and what you'll notice here is that

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:27.100
one intuition is that the
network is more responsive

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.400
to line than it is to tone,
and that's one theory.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.430
And this might explain
why it doesn't matter

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:34.560
if the boxer is facing left or right,

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:35.930
nor does it matter if the boxer

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:38.840
is Caucasian or African
American, now there may

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.370
or may not be an explicit
category in the Beinecke's

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.490
own expert cataloging
of the Meserve-Kunhardt

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:47.110
Collection of pugilist, but
it doesn't really matter

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.810
because we've built a
virtual subject category

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:51.160
by relying on the conventions

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:53.410
or portraiture in this
particular collection.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.130
So that's Neural Neighbors
and that's open-source

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:58.630
software that people
can use, in fact we're

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:01.460
pushing this tool forward
to help solve some problems

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:02.780
that might be familiar for those who know

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:05.490
about the Farrows
Collection of photography

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.030
and we're also pushing that

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:08.300
in a couple different directions.

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:10.810
We wanna make this available
for anybody to use,

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:12.838
it's all open science, we
didn't invent these algorithms

00:14:12.838 --> 00:14:15.360
we just sort of put it together.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.400
And we also wanted to go further,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.620
and so the second technique
I'm gonna talk to you about

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:22.680
actually builds on the
idea of I have one image

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:24.500
and I wanna see everything
which is similar

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:26.340
to it in my collection.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:28.840
Using that intuition of
using pre-trained networks,

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:30.800
but stepping back from the final category

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.480
and going to a semi-final
layer which is more abstract,

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.580
can we use that more abstract space

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:39.800
as a way of organizing the
entire collection en mas.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:41.790
So this gets to what I
was talking about earlier,

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:43.690
the collection-scale analysis,

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:45.850
or collection-scale visualization.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:48.000
And so we have another
software product out there

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.200
called PixPlot, and it actually
uses some of the same math

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:53.200
that we just talked about,
pre-trained networks,

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:56.290
discard the layers which
are optimized for 2019,

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:59.220
take the penultimate layer,
and then we have the same

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:01.220
dimensionality reduction problem.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:05.100
We need to somehow
visualize 2,048 dimensions

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:07.010
in our web browser and
that's really tough to do

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:09.880
with two-dimensional screens,
so dimensionality reduction

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:12.120
is basically like the
idea of taking a flower

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:14.550
and pressing it between
the leaves of a book.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.100
You as a human are able to figure out,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:17.970
how best can I crush this flower

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:20.770
and still preserve its distinctive shape.

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:22.410
It's pretty easy for a
human to do that from

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.060
three dimensions to two dimensions.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:26.810
To do it from 2,048 dimensions
down to two dimensions

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:29.210
takes more math, and there are
a whole bunch of techniques,

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.680
something as simple as
principle component analysis,

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.350
something more advanced like t
Stochastic Neighbor Embedding

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:38.580
or tSNE and then a really cool,
new algorithm called UMAP,

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:41.130
Uniform Manifold
Approximation and Projection,

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.550
they're always a minimizing loss,

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:45.750
minimizing what you're
destroying by crushing

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:48.360
that flower from three
dimensions to two dimensions.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:50.450
And now we have a two-dimensional space

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:51.890
if we're successful in this reduction

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:53.500
and we somehow now have to show you

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.350
a lot of images all at the same time.

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:57.800
I'll show you how this works here.

00:15:57.800 --> 00:15:59.680
And for this demo I thought maybe with

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:02.590
the permission of Manuel
to show some images

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:04.250
from the Yale Center for British Art.

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:06.340
So we've taken about 31,000 images

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:08.320
as we know the Yale Center for British Art

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:12.210
was a pioneer in putting
non-copyright encumbered images

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:13.940
online for anybody to work with

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:16.350
and so we benefit from that generosity

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:18.640
and I'm able to take all of
those images they gave me

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:20.140
and throw them on the floor.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:22.630
But they haven't landed randomly.

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:24.680
They've landed in such a way that images

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:27.490
that share the same visual characteristics

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:29.340
have landed on the
floor at the same place.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.520
So I'm sure there's a
technical word for these,

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:33.770
I call them the caricatures of dandies.

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:36.230
These are all the
well-dressed men in very nice

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:39.960
clothing down here at the
bottom left of my visualization.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.010
This visualization is in
fact the result of a UMAP

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:46.240
production of 2,048 dimensions
into two dimensions.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.180
And what I'm doing here
is showing this reduction

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:52.320
and hopefully UMAP has
been effective at keeping

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.850
images that look like
each other near each other

00:16:54.850 --> 00:16:56.883
in this much reduced dimensional space.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:00.120
It isn't just these guys, we also have,

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:02.797
anybody here like animals,
let's go to the zoo,

00:17:02.797 --> 00:17:05.140
here's the zoo, and it's everybody

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:08.760
from horses to camels and
things like that on my screen.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:10.840
There are some other
really interesting things

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:14.330
which show characteristics of YCBA data.

00:17:14.330 --> 00:17:16.680
All the vignetted sketches.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.630
My favorite one may be since we have

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:20.310
some natural scientists in the room,

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:21.490
is that there's this really cool

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:23.790
cluster of botanical illustration.

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:26.060
I'm sure there's a category
for this in the explicit

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:28.250
metadata at YCBA, I just
think it's kinda cool

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:30.763
that this thing just all
landed in the same place.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:35.060
And then finally, there's
some cool stuff down here.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:37.120
The Yale Center for British Art of course

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:39.660
justifiably considers frames to be artwork

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:40.890
in and of themself, so we have

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:42.780
some really terrific frames here.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:45.120
What I love about his is
these images of frames

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:47.650
are close to this other cluster of images

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.600
where the frame is too
big, really in my opinion,

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:52.300
so it's Frameville, right,
we're in Frameville.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.050
But we have this enormous visualization.

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:56.150
People sometimes use
metaphors of this looks like

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:59.800
a constellation, it looks
like an archipelago of islands

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:02.420
and things like that, but
this is what anybody can do

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:04.040
if you load your pictures into PixPlot.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.930
We recommend at least 5 to 10,000 images.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:09.793
We are pushing PixPlot forward
in some interesting ways.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:12.700
It is primarily authored by Doug Duhaime

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:15.040
and so I do want to acknowledge his work

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:17.510
in programming this in
WebGL which is a way

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:19.790
of programming websites
that's more akin to writing

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:22.510
a 3D video than using HTML tags.

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:23.710
That's the reason we're able to get

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:25.553
really high performance out of this.

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:29.200
But what I wanna do is to
talk about another example

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:30.470
from my own research, I'm gonna

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.550
give you about 30,000 pictures from

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:36.210
the Southern Swedish city
of Lund in Scandinavia

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:37.850
and so we're gonna have these images

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:39.760
which were taken by two photographers

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:43.320
just to show you how this
works on photography.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:45.410
So we have these unexpected clusters.

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:47.630
These are all sort of from
the early 20th century.

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:50.510
Turns out people liked taking
pictures of kids in chairs

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:52.080
in the early 20th century in Sweden.

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:53.610
These are all these kids in chairs.

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:55.610
I had no idea this was a genre,

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:57.810
and it's nearby the cluster
of standing children,

00:18:57.810 --> 00:18:59.500
so I guess as a parent you could decide

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.450
do I want my kid standing
up for their portrait

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:03.890
or do I want them in a chair?

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:06.080
There's other examples
here, turns out marriages

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:08.500
cohere pretty well, these
are wedding pictures, right?

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:10.370
There's women in white gowns.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:12.240
And there's also something kind of cool

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.220
I wanted to show you which
is we've got pictures

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:16.310
of buildings here in
the center of my screen,

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:18.050
pictures of parks down here,

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:21.400
and what's between buildings and parks,

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:24.220
turns out to be buildings in parks.

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:26.610
So there was originally
probably one dimension

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:29.480
which was responding to the
grass and trees in parks

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.140
and there was another
dimension that was responding

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:34.390
to the linearity, the roof
lines, the walls of houses

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:39.300
or buildings and UMAP, a
dimensionality reduction algorithm

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:43.570
was able to somehow
collapse these two lines

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:46.773
into a much smaller space that
still preserves the spectrum.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:50.370
I wanna show you a really
early alpha version of PixPlot

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.910
which accomplishes something
even more exciting.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:54.930
In this demo is going to be showing you

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:57.600
the interrelationship
between human metadata

00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:58.850
and what the computer sees.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:00.720
So of course when I was dealing with the

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:02.830
Yale Center for British Art images,

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.390
I was not taking into account who

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.670
painted that or who drew that.

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:08.440
And what we're doing with
the new version of PixPlot

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:10.980
is being able to pull these
explicit metadata edges

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:13.240
like who drew this, who wrote this,

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:15.750
who painted it, what year was it,

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:17.280
and put that into conversation with

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:20.320
some of our visualization, so
the first thing I'll show you

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:22.880
is that we do have the
ability to think about these

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:25.440
images in a variety of
dimensionality reductions,

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.880
this is just the most
banal, this is just all

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:30.400
the images one by one by
one, that's pretty cool.

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:32.260
But before we were showing you

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:34.460
a couple different
dimensionality reductions,

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:36.520
now I can show you the
animation between them.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:38.450
So we might have for example these images,

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.170
which are about 30,000
orienting themselves

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:43.533
into a t-SNE reduction, which is

00:20:43.533 --> 00:20:45.550
just one way of organizing them.

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:47.820
We can do that with
another dimension added in,

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:49.850
and you're seeing about 30,000 objects

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:52.800
move all at the same time
on your screen in WebGL.

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.380
T-SNE has managed to orient these down

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:56.710
in a way that here's the neighborhood

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:58.140
of those kids in chairs, so we still

00:20:58.140 --> 00:20:59.740
have that clustering, right?

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:01.640
Each algorithm will put the
stuff in different parts

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:04.460
of your screen and there is
a stochastic or random aspect

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:07.410
to each visualization,
so we don't want people

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:10.340
to take these as
deterministic, we just want

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:11.730
them to take them as suggestive,

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:13.430
and in fact that's one of
the reasons we've moved

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:15.170
towards this animation engine.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:17.220
So that people can see that
all of these arrangements

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.470
are really contingent, and
there are different ways

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:22.160
of thinking about the data
that are all legitimate.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:25.070
This is UMAP, one of our most exciting

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:27.430
visualizations 'cause it
certainly clusters things

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:30.530
in a certain way, but I think
what I wanted to show you here

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:33.440
was this interrelationship
between the metadata,

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:36.240
who took this picture, and the algorithm,

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:39.180
which sees the notion
of children in chairs.

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:41.370
So what I can do with
this version of PixPlot

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:44.120
is I can say turn off a
particular photographer.

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:46.379
Only show me pictures by Per Bagge

00:21:46.379 --> 00:21:48.170
or only show me pictures by Ida Ekelund.

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:50.030
Now when I do that most of the images

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:52.160
of children in chairs disappear.

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:54.670
There's still this cluster
of children in chairs,

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:57.860
but if I turn off Per Bagge,
and only show you Ida Ekelund

00:21:57.860 --> 00:21:59.560
then what I get is a much reduced cluster

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:01.180
whereas if I switched to Per Bagge,

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:03.420
here indeed are all the kids in chairs.

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.230
And so what this tells you is there's

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:06.390
a substantial difference between

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:08.740
the output of these two
photographers who worked in

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:12.430
the same building and trained
with the same woman in Lund,

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:13.840
and they were active at the same time,

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:15.840
but this woman did not
shoot babies in chairs,

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:17.030
that wasn't her bag.

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:19.880
She actually shot a lot of images

00:22:20.862 --> 00:22:23.350
of women in their 20s and
30s with very short hair

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:25.410
and in the American context
you would think of these people

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:28.090
as flappers, so there's a
separate cluster of flappers

00:22:28.090 --> 00:22:30.400
that only Ida Ekelund took pictures of.

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:31.830
That's one of the things
that we can discover

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:33.930
when we put machine vision in conversation

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.300
with the explicit
metadata edges of who took

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:38.300
this picture, and when did they take it.

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:43.240
Another exciting thing
about the future of PixPlot

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:46.470
is that we want to turn this
into a tool for curators.

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:48.450
Pretend we were going to that zoo area

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:49.990
we were looking at before, and you say,

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:52.060
hey, I'm really interested in horses,

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:53.980
so I'm gonna trust the
algorithm to cluster

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:55.470
all the animals together 'cause it seems

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:57.030
to do a pretty good job of that,

00:22:57.030 --> 00:22:59.550
but I don't want any
camels in my collection

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:01.460
'cause I'm preparing an exhibit on horses.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:04.553
And so what we can do is let
you have a kind of lasso,

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:08.250
remember you're in Photoshop or Paint

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:11.220
and you're trying draw a
complex shape around something,

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:14.100
so what we'll allow you
to do is draw your own

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:16.620
circle or your own lasso shape around

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.210
just the horses that you like, you'll be

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:20.320
able to Shift + select, deselect.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:21.840
And then what do you do with that stuff,

00:23:21.840 --> 00:23:24.950
well you might download it as a CSV file,

00:23:24.950 --> 00:23:26.920
you might bounce it into a PowerPoint,

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:28.750
you might upload it to Omeka,

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:30.790
you might just have a list of things

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:32.400
you need to take a closer look at.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:35.560
So being able to put
the human intuition into

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:38.230
a conversation with a way
that the machine vision

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:41.113
is organizing these images
we think is really exciting.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:44.790
We wanna work with human
expertise not against it.

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:46.390
I think that's my main takeaway.

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:49.380
I have five minutes left and now I wanna

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:50.980
show you something kind of edgy.

00:23:53.049 --> 00:23:53.882
I like to think of this as if,

00:23:53.882 --> 00:23:56.180
have you ever been working
on a book manuscript

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:58.240
or an article or a student evaluation?

00:23:58.240 --> 00:23:59.470
And you've been working on this

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:00.860
for like every day this week,

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:03.160
your book or your article or whatever,

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:04.750
maybe you're reorganizing your closet.

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:06.560
And it's all you can do all day,

00:24:06.560 --> 00:24:08.610
and when you go to bed
there's this horrible thing

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:10.180
when you start dreaming about the chapter

00:24:10.180 --> 00:24:11.150
you're trying to write during

00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:12.890
the day, it will not leave you.

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:15.400
And we were thinking that
we're making some of this

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:17.300
Nvidia hardware and these convolutional

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:19.730
neural network software
work really hard all day.

00:24:19.730 --> 00:24:22.540
We run these jobs for seven
or eight days in a row,

00:24:22.540 --> 00:24:24.640
multiple GPUS in the cube over there.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:26.970
And we thought well, what happens when

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:28.490
these neural networks go off the shift

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:30.820
when it's five p.m.,
what do they dream about?

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:32.900
Do they start hallucinating about all

00:24:32.900 --> 00:24:35.390
these portraits they've
been seeing during the day?

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:37.420
And so what we wanted to do was explore

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:39.150
the notion of generative models.

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:41.270
And generative models are of course

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:43.930
a form of unsupervised machine learning

00:24:43.930 --> 00:24:46.140
and the notion is that given enough

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:49.100
ground truth, given enough training data

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:51.393
of things as they are in the world,

00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:55.090
algorithms might be able
to come to an understanding

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:57.770
of a probability distribution
of the characters

00:24:57.770 --> 00:25:00.590
in the words of a book or the pixel values

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:02.400
in a 19th century portrait.

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:05.160
And we might be able to
sample from these networks

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:06.820
and produce new objects which have

00:25:06.820 --> 00:25:09.410
never existed in the world before.

00:25:09.410 --> 00:25:11.630
If you read in the Times
or the Washington Post

00:25:11.630 --> 00:25:13.310
articles about deepfakes, that's actually

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:15.940
what's going on here,
they're generative models.

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:18.560
And so what I'm gonna show you today,

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:20.550
a notion of generative
adversarial networks.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:22.370
Now the cool thing about
generative adversarial networks

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:25.880
is actually two networks working in tandem

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:29.100
or more accurately working
adversarial to one another.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:30.880
Generative adversarial networks

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:34.120
consist of two networks, an art forger

00:25:34.120 --> 00:25:37.710
who is shown images of
10s of 1000s of objects

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:42.710
and does its best to invent
or hallucinate new things.

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:46.090
There's a joke here about
then selling the forged object

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:47.190
to the Getty here, which I won't make

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:48.960
'cause we have somebody in the Getty here.

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:52.070
The detective though is
working for the art museum

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:55.360
and he or she is looking
at all these objects

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:57.920
and trying to discern which
one actually existed in

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:01.630
the real world and which ones
were dreamt up by the forger.

00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:04.150
And what's interesting
about this interrelation

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:06.530
is that each of these two neural networks

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:08.700
pitted against each
other in an adversarial

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:12.140
relationship learns from its mistakes.

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:16.060
If the forger does not get an
image through the detective

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:18.340
then it says, well I screwed
up and I gotta do better.

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:20.470
Let me go back to the ground
truth and try to learn

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:22.980
a better prior, a better
probability distribution

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:25.990
of RGB pixels or characters.

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:27.960
If the detective accidentally lets

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:31.140
a fake Greek sculpture
through it then says

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:33.210
I should never do that
again, let me revisit

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:36.060
my ideas about what is
real, and what is fake,

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:39.770
and all this happens 10s
of 1000s times a second

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:43.490
and we run these for
days and days and days.

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:45.320
So this is what happens when we ran

00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:46.660
a generative adversarial network

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:48.170
on the Meserve-Kunhardt Collection

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:49.940
of faces from this 19th century,

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:52.150
Civil War, Gilded Age collection.

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:54.570
What you're seeing is the
output of the generator

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:56.300
fighting against the discriminator

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:58.870
trying to arrive at some
kind of consensus on what

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:01.470
a 19th century face looks like.

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:04.430
And now some people when they
see this they think it is

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:06.860
uncanny, some people think
it's like a horror movie

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:09.100
or something, but you're
seeing in real time,

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:11.450
the result of two neural networks fighting

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:13.540
each other over the definition of a face.

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:15.170
It doesn't understand what a face is.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:18.903
But that's how we interpret as humans.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:22.560
You'll sometimes see faces
that are split in half.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:24.520
I have another example here I'll show you,

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:26.250
this is the same algorithm running

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:28.260
those Swedish pictures
I showed you earlier.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:30.410
And these are about 30,000 faces.

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:33.320
And so you'll see these two
networks fighting each other

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:35.750
and what you'll find there on the left

00:27:35.750 --> 00:27:37.130
if you look on the
right side of the screen

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:40.260
I have some samples
drawn from the results.

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:42.980
Early on in the process,
what we call an early epoch,

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:44.540
you'll see static, I mean this is

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:46.220
no better than just random pixels.

00:27:46.220 --> 00:27:48.150
And that's because the generator

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:49.790
at the very beginning
doesn't know what it's doing.

00:27:49.790 --> 00:27:51.300
It's like, well, have this page

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:52.940
of static and see if that works.

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:55.300
Of course the discriminator,
although not expert yet

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:57.790
is good enough to
determine this is no good.

00:27:57.790 --> 00:27:59.150
The middle picture is actually

00:27:59.150 --> 00:28:01.290
I think an example of a good example

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:03.400
in the sense that this woman never lived,

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:04.870
but if you looked at
this image, this face,

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:07.060
you might think ah, it's just a lossy JPEG

00:28:07.060 --> 00:28:09.320
face of a Swedish woman, not a problem.

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:11.200
And then on the far
right we have an example

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:13.860
of maybe overcompensation
or some kind of mistake

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:16.120
the generator made which
said, I got a face through

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:18.330
with two eyes and one
nose, let me try three eyes

00:28:18.330 --> 00:28:20.010
and two noses and see if that works.

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:22.020
I hope to God the discriminator realized

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:24.273
that was not a real
human, but you never know.

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:27.890
So what can we learn from
these, well not much.

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:30.120
These are kind of in the realm of art,

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:32.570
but I think that it is
interesting to compare

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:35.900
ho algorithms come to understand
probability distributions

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:38.410
of RGB pixel values when we ourselves

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:39.830
spend so much time thinking

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:41.660
and looking at these same images.

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:43.630
Looking at the uncanny or horrific

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:46.420
is sometimes a way of casting new light

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:48.070
on the work we do with the authentic.

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:50.950
And I do think there is
possibly some role here

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:53.420
for curators and other professionals

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:55.040
to work with these types of things

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:57.890
at least as ways of
understanding some of the aspects

00:28:57.890 --> 00:29:00.470
of the possibilities of machine vision

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:03.540
when it goes off work
and it starts to dream.

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:05.000
Promising areas for the future,

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:07.410
I think that we do need
to appropriate algorithms

00:29:07.410 --> 00:29:09.360
from private industry,
I think we need to build

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:12.740
up expertise within
GLAM for machine vision.

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:15.120
I think private partnerships
are also really crucial

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:17.370
but I'd love for if Google came knocking

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:20.860
I'd love for people on
Yale's campus to bring

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:24.900
not only their existing
expertise, but also

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:28.090
some basic knowledge of what
these algorithms can do.

00:29:28.090 --> 00:29:31.510
I think combining collections
across institutions

00:29:31.510 --> 00:29:33.380
for example reassembling Meserve-Kunhardt

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:35.480
between NPG and Beinecke.

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:37.830
Placing human curatorial
and disciplinary knowledge

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:39.370
as equal partners to machine vision.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:41.920
We're starting to do some
baby steps towards that

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:43.860
with being able to show metadata facets

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:47.900
in our visualizations,
rearrange to suggest multitude

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:50.430
and complexity rather than
deterministic simplicity

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:52.770
and let you go in with that
lasso and select things

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:54.610
that are meaningful to you.

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:56.560
And finally and most
crucially, if you listen

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:58.810
to one thing I've said today, it's that

00:29:58.810 --> 00:30:01.580
institutions such as Yale and Smithsonian

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:04.110
are actually uniquely suited to produce

00:30:04.110 --> 00:30:06.420
captioning models, descriptive models

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:08.350
that do match the world of bumblebees,

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:12.040
that do match the 1860s
because we have the excellent

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:14.513
metadata and we have the training data

00:30:14.513 --> 00:30:16.570
and all the metadata of the raster pixels

00:30:16.570 --> 00:30:18.905
of our own collections,
rather than getting upset

00:30:18.905 --> 00:30:21.010
that something is
mischaracterized in an image,

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:23.140
we can retrain the last
layer in these networks

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:25.300
and produce our own 19th century models

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:28.050
of American visual culture, I
think that's very promising.

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:30.059
So with that let me end.

00:30:30.059 --> 00:30:32.143
We'll save questions for the lunch.

